# ----- 1+2 Template Architecture -----

built-in: inheritance, include, partials

django-cotton           https://django-cotton.com/
slippers                https://mitchel.me/slippers/introduction/
django-web-components   https://github.com/Xzya/django-web-components

django-components       https://github.com/django-components/django-components

BOTTOMLINE:
- independent of styling & dynamic interactions (HTMX, Alpine.js) -> can all interchangeably be integrated
- cotton/slippers/web-components are very much alike, all used to define structured and reusable components,
    but cotton seems to be most popular/simple
- built-in django features (especially partials) can do just as well as cotton if well organized
- django-components used for defining components in back-end python code and directly including css/js there,
    probably highest ceiling but also rather complex
- transition, for example from partials to cotton, would be possible later

-> use plain django (with partials and include) for now, but structure it in a component-like way
    to be able to switch to cotton or even django-components later


# ----- 3+4 Styling -----

CSS/SCSS
    - traditional design concept, write classes yourself

Bootstrap/Bulma/Foundation
    - predefined classes/styles, can be customized
    - many more available, though not sure what else integrated well with django,
        see: https://zairalabs.ai/guide/guides/css-ui-compared/
    - not just CSS/styling, but also interactive components like dropdown menus

Tailwind CSS
    - middle ground between CSS (write it yourself) and Bootstrap/others (give me predefined, styled components)
    - other libraries with predefined components on top of tailwind (for example DaisyUI, Flowbite)
    - main alternative: UnoCSS (more flexible than Tailwind, see: https://dev.to/mapleleaf/tailwindcss-vs-unocss-2a53)
    - other alternatives: Twind, Typewind, MasterCSS

CSS Modules    https://css-tricks.com/css-modules-part-1-need/
    dynamically generated classes, unique, mapped to the correct styles (local scope by default)

Design Tokens   https://css-tricks.com/what-are-design-tokens/
    method to store design variables (to be used in css for example) in one place in order to use it
    across platforms and projects

Component libraries can be built by combining a structured template architecture with styling,
    eg. generating predefined buttons, modals and so on with cotton and tailwind

bunch of options for responsive design incl.:
tailwind responsive utilities
media queries - responsive to screen size
container queries - responsive container size (for example a button is rendered differently
    depending whether it's in a small navbar or in the middle of a big block)
-> media queries and container queries are both linked to native CSS,
    but doesnt make them mutually exclusive with tailwind

BOTTOMLINE:
- use Tailwind for now


# ----- 5-8 Interactive behaviour -----

most important distinction:
what happens on the server and what happens solely on the browser (client-side)?

with templates:
- server runs python code and is connected to database
- browser GETs something from the server
- browser renders templates and runs javascript for interactive things like dropdown menus
- browser POSTs something back to the server


currently a lot of stuff in the old ogp templates is based on bootstrap
    (which does styling and javascript interactions within the browser)

more sophisticated things for the new architecture:
HTMX -> used for server-browser-communication, shortcuts some GET and POST requests

Alpine.js -> simple javascript interactivity, directly added into html templates
Stimulus.js -> keeps javascript and html separated, slightly less intuitive but
                potentially cleaner if complexity of interactions grows
    https://unitysangam.com/tech/alpine-js-vs-stimulus-js/
    https://dev.to/alex_aslam/alpinejs-vs-stimulus-a-pragmatic-comparison-ake

Vanilla javascript can always be added to program some very specific interactions

React / Vue / Svelte
    ...and many many more for more complex javascript stuff

BOTTOMLINE:
- use alpine.js and maybe add some custom javascript or others on top if needed
